**`parseNote(from:)` function** (lines 14–26):
```swift
public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note {
    let parts = text.split(separator: "\n---\n")
    guard parts.count == 4 else {
        throw NoteParseError.malformedFormat
    }
    return Note(
        id: String(parts[0]),
        title: String(parts[1]),
        pinned: String(parts[2]) == "1",
        body: String(parts[3]),
        tags: extractTags(from: String(parts[3]))
    )
}
```

Key design points:
- Splits on `\n---\n` (the exact Rust CLI separator). If the split doesn't produce exactly 4 parts, throws `malformedFormat` — no guessing, no defaults, no crashing.
- `pinned` is `true` only when part[2] is exactly `"1"`.
- `tags` are extracted from the body (part[3]) using the existing `extractTags(from:)`.
- No existing code was touched — only appended before the `Note` struct.

